WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.380
Welcome to Summit One.

00:00:01.820 --> 00:00:05.000
A huge number, a hidden pattern, a puzzling shape:

00:00:05.520 --> 00:00:07.200
each is an invitation to think.

00:00:07.900 --> 00:00:09.580
Here you'll build the habits and methods

00:00:09.580 --> 00:00:13.440
to tackle unfamiliar Australian Mathematics Competition problems

00:00:13.440 --> 00:00:14.220
with confidence.

00:00:15.530 --> 00:00:19.030
Upper Primary is the AMC division for Years Five and Six.

00:00:19.650 --> 00:00:21.650
You have sixty minutes for thirty questions,

00:00:21.910 --> 00:00:23.110
on paper or online.

00:00:23.670 --> 00:00:26.290
The questions generally become more challenging as you go.

00:00:27.320 --> 00:00:30.000
Questions one to twenty-five are multiple choice:

00:00:30.760 --> 00:00:32.260
choose one answer from A to E.

00:00:32.780 --> 00:00:34.380
The final five need a whole number

00:00:34.380 --> 00:00:36.280
from zero to nine hundred and ninety-nine.

00:00:37.180 --> 00:00:38.160
You supply the number.

00:00:39.160 --> 00:00:40.840
Marks rise through the paper:

00:00:41.240 --> 00:00:42.640
three each for the first ten,

00:00:42.880 --> 00:00:45.100
four for the next ten, then five each.

00:00:45.740 --> 00:00:49.800
The final five are worth six, seven, eight, nine and ten marks.

00:00:50.440 --> 00:00:52.180
That's one hundred and thirty-five altogether.

00:00:53.760 --> 00:00:55.500
Wrong answers do not lose marks.

00:00:56.220 --> 00:00:57.700
Start with questions you can solve,

00:00:58.040 --> 00:00:59.400
then return to tougher ones.

00:01:00.020 --> 00:01:01.580
Leave time to check missing answers.

00:01:02.380 --> 00:01:03.200
Under the current rules,

00:01:03.500 --> 00:01:06.900
pocket or scientific calculators are allowed in Upper Primary.

00:01:07.580 --> 00:01:09.060
Careful reasoning still matters.

00:01:10.140 --> 00:01:13.180
Problems can involve number, fractions and ratios,

00:01:13.740 --> 00:01:15.700
algebra, geometry and measurement,

00:01:15.920 --> 00:01:18.060
statistics, probability and counting.

00:01:18.860 --> 00:01:20.920
You'll learn to notice the mathematical structure

00:01:20.920 --> 00:01:23.720
underneath the story, and choose a method that fits.

00:01:24.810 --> 00:01:25.790
Take number patterns.

00:01:26.550 --> 00:01:28.130
What is the final digit of three

00:01:28.130 --> 00:01:29.350
to the power of one hundred and twenty-three?

00:01:30.930 --> 00:01:34.670
The last digits repeat: three, nine, seven, one.

00:01:34.670 --> 00:01:36.650
Four positions make a cycle.

00:01:37.710 --> 00:01:40.650
One hundred and twenty-three is thirty groups of four,

00:01:40.650 --> 00:01:42.090
with three left over.

00:01:42.570 --> 00:01:44.490
So use the third position: seven.

00:01:44.970 --> 00:01:46.810
A remainder turns an enormous calculation

00:01:46.810 --> 00:01:48.210
into a small decision.

00:01:49.690 --> 00:01:51.910
Ratios become clearer as equal parts.

00:01:52.650 --> 00:01:55.210
Blue and gold counters are in the ratio three to five.

00:01:55.670 --> 00:01:56.870
There are twelve more gold.

00:01:57.490 --> 00:02:00.070
The difference is two parts, so each part is six.

00:02:00.770 --> 00:02:02.630
Altogether, eight parts make forty-eight.

00:02:04.230 --> 00:02:06.230
Geometry rewards seeing structure.

00:02:07.050 --> 00:02:08.270
In a three by four grid,

00:02:08.490 --> 00:02:09.850
how many rectangles are there?

00:02:10.370 --> 00:02:12.190
Choose two of the four horizontal lines

00:02:12.190 --> 00:02:14.150
and two of the five vertical lines.

00:02:14.850 --> 00:02:17.530
Six choices times ten gives sixty rectangles.

00:02:19.030 --> 00:02:20.810
Five is subtracted from a number,

00:02:21.290 --> 00:02:24.590
the result is doubled, then seven is added, giving thirty-one.

00:02:25.390 --> 00:02:26.410
Undo the steps in reverse:

00:02:27.230 --> 00:02:29.530
subtract seven, halve, then add five.

00:02:30.070 --> 00:02:31.390
The starting number is seventeen.

00:02:32.830 --> 00:02:35.170
Counting and probability need organised cases.

00:02:35.930 --> 00:02:38.230
Roll two fair six-sided dice.

00:02:38.950 --> 00:02:40.730
Six ordered outcomes add to seven,

00:02:41.190 --> 00:02:43.430
from thirty-six equally likely outcomes.

00:02:44.010 --> 00:02:45.750
The probability is one sixth.

00:02:46.050 --> 00:02:48.870
A table helps you count every case exactly once.

00:02:50.410 --> 00:02:53.870
Can fifteen counters split into two equal whole-number groups?

00:02:54.290 --> 00:02:55.970
Pair them: one is left over.

00:02:56.410 --> 00:02:57.110
This is parity.

00:02:57.730 --> 00:02:59.690
Along with colouring and things that stay unchanged,

00:03:00.370 --> 00:03:01.950
it can rule out impossible answers.

00:03:02.570 --> 00:03:03.750
You'll learn to explain why.

00:03:04.910 --> 00:03:06.350
Start a lesson with Read.

00:03:07.050 --> 00:03:10.050
Try the small example on paper before you open a step.

00:03:10.630 --> 00:03:13.030
If you get stuck, reveal the first step.

00:03:13.550 --> 00:03:16.670
Then uncover the rest and see where your method matches.

00:03:17.820 --> 00:03:19.700
On Watch, follow the moving diagram.

00:03:20.480 --> 00:03:21.820
Think about the rectangle example.

00:03:22.320 --> 00:03:25.600
Choose two horizontal boundaries and two vertical boundaries.

00:03:26.220 --> 00:03:28.000
Their intersections mark one rectangle.

00:03:28.600 --> 00:03:29.880
Pause to predict the next move,

00:03:29.880 --> 00:03:32.440
or replay a chapter to see how it works.

00:03:33.860 --> 00:03:37.340
In Practise, work on paper and enter your final answer.

00:03:38.020 --> 00:03:40.320
Check it, then compare your working with the solution.

00:03:41.080 --> 00:03:44.000
If you used a hint or someone helped, record that too.

00:03:44.500 --> 00:03:47.260
For a missed answer, find the first step you would change.

00:03:48.420 --> 00:03:52.060
In the Test Centre, Part A covers questions one to twenty.

00:03:52.660 --> 00:03:54.100
The clock counts up from zero.

00:03:54.820 --> 00:03:55.940
Aim for every answer correct

00:03:55.940 --> 00:03:57.340
in twenty minutes or less.

00:03:58.080 --> 00:04:00.400
Part B covers questions twenty-one to thirty.

00:04:00.880 --> 00:04:02.220
It has a forty-minute limit.

00:04:02.880 --> 00:04:04.720
After either part, open your report

00:04:04.720 --> 00:04:05.840
to review your answers.

00:04:07.140 --> 00:04:08.780
You don't need every answer today.

00:04:09.400 --> 00:04:12.440
Start with a clue, draw a picture, try a small case,

00:04:12.760 --> 00:04:13.640
and build from there.

00:04:14.180 --> 00:04:16.440
Each thoughtful attempt strengthens your toolkit.

00:04:16.840 --> 00:04:17.540
Let's begin.
